#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188193 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/id #uuid "ddfa9789-7019-5939-b675-bcef59e5ef4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15151" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p,\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $120$ apricots from a population of $20\\,000$, where $75\\%$ of the apricots weigh more than $50$ g.\n\nII. A sample of $25$ gym members from a population containing $200$ members, where $80\\%$ of the gym's members work out for more than $100$ minutes each visit.\n\nIII. A sample of $45$ flights in an airport, given that $90\\%$ of flights arrive on time. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\leq 5\\% \\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =120 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 20\\,000 \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ np & =120(0.75) \\\\  & =90 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120(1-0.75) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <25.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied. \n\nFor the other conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =45(0.9) \\\\  & =40.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =45(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p) \\ngtr 5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188194 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" :question/math-academy-id "e-15152" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9292 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9306 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9319 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9418 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9429 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9441 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9525 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9535 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9545 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 84,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = 0.42.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 84(0.42) = 35.28 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 84(1-0.42) = 48.72 > 5,\n$$\n\nand $n=84$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{84}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,0.0029).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p} > 0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.5-0.42}{\\sqrt{0.0029}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.49) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.49) & =0.9319.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =1-Φ(1.49) \\\\  & =1-0.9319 \\\\  & =0.0681.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188195 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" :question/math-academy-id "e-15153" :question/problem "A popular streaming service has a database of $100$ million songs, of which $64$ million have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. If a user randomly creates a playlist with $128$ songs, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $64$ and $96$ (inclusive) songs in the sample have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.4 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.3 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0006 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.07 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.5 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9949 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9951 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9952 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9962 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9963 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9964 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9972 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9973 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9974 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 128,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = \\dfrac{64\\,000\\,000}{100\\,000\\,000} = 0.64.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 128(0.64) = 81.92 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 128(1-0.64) = 46.08 > 5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=128$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,0.0018).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $64$ and $96$ of the songs in the playlist last less than $3$ minutes, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{64}{128}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{96}{128} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5 \\leq          \\widehat{\\,p} \\leq          0.75).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.75-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-3.30\\le Z\\le 2.59) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.59)-P(Z\\le -3.30) \\\\  & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.59)=0.9952,\\,Φ(-3.30)=0.0005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30) \\\\  & =0.9952-0.0005 \\\\  & =0.9947.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2dfdd82-65a5-5171-bc01-7821fbf0698e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186367} {:answer/id #uuid "c39133e9-a888-5d29-b93c-9e5408b6d72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3853" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186368} {:answer/id #uuid "1356a3ec-56fc-5170-816e-abe9552e7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186369} {:answer/id #uuid "2abc7d34-8251-5017-899a-0156b06b5501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186370} {:answer/id #uuid "7e69b089-b6f5-5b27-9c28-5a5089665d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0835" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1356a3ec-56fc-5170-816e-abe9552e7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186369} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8ca12c-8970-56fc-9321-068eac62971b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da01a854-bda8-5ccb-8dc9-1deaf2392385" :question/math-academy-id "q-146160" :question/problem "In a school of $900$ students, $432$ of the students are male. If the school randomly samples $50$ students, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $20$ and $23$ (inclusive) of students in the sample are male. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{432}{900}=0.48.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.48)=24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.48)=26>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,\\frac{0.48(1-0.48)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,0.0050).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $20$ and $23$ of students in the sample are male, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{50}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{23}{50} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.46-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.13\\le Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.28)-P(Z\\le -1.13) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.28)=0.3897,\\,Φ(-1.13)=0.1292.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.46) & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13) \\\\  & =0.3897-0.1292 \\\\  & =0.2605.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3601da7-a7c3-52cd-8f70-f617dbff7130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186391} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc85bb-7f5c-57a0-98aa-12945003cf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186392} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d07aea-24ea-5f53-9672-04c6dad6b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186393} {:answer/id #uuid "68126574-febc-56bd-96e3-be660cd1d396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186394} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c475d-4fb2-5d19-8a64-6b6d5fd924f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3601da7-a7c3-52cd-8f70-f617dbff7130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186391} :answer-field/id #uuid "0083b82a-9efc-51d0-860f-723f4347222c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6270c9-4286-526b-a383-94a2b499f6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146169" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=16$ from a population of size $N=100$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=32$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=32$  from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =16,\\, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & <16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.1) \\\\  & =3.2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.75) \\\\  & =24 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.75) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4ff370-88d6-5364-ac58-0e5d98900a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501519} {:answer/id #uuid "9a40a784-700c-5e4c-9e97-c0835f0a60b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501757} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bf5c83-f1eb-519c-8394-47157850924b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501781} {:answer/id #uuid "d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501888} {:answer/id #uuid "f616020e-49fd-5757-a8e6-0c21672f2fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/re