ements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4116f3f0-c977-5e04-8b37-cfee23a31d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501586} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501627} {:answer/id #uuid "619c4398-31f6-54ae-99e8-d78d34165a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac32c0bc-5323-5faa-b8d5-33267bce243a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501808} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ba3ea6-389e-54cf-a4c9-3ac81a2a28ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501627} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00782d6-c22d-5c54-843e-daefb08e60d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" :question/math-academy-id "q-146838" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56867d82-3d07-5414-9450-8f0c79240546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.78)-Φ(-0.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501624} {:answer/id #uuid "68727b43-f29b-53a1-a5d7-6dcd49a3a034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.36)-Φ(-0.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501657} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbdab0-ba6b-5637-9d3a-42a4f9671754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.96)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501782} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9cff08-4800-5f04-8bf4-570544c083f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.80)-Φ(-0.80)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501801} {:answer/id #uuid "fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db1ac1a-fba9-53f0-abaa-ce90a089ca8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" :question/math-academy-id "q-146942" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15%$ and $25%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff494bd-0b9f-5135-9d18-50e3d2df3439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.71)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501549} {:answer/id #uuid "50ba5d47-3502-5e34-8efb-9b1500551a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.79)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501613} {:answer/id #uuid "729c9ecc-6c95-5e5f-ad08-f8c44fba093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501676} {:answer/id #uuid "8b101809-2ccd-5ced-a664-5deefedab9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501732} {:answer/id #uuid "9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.87)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.87)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b081ec7e-333d-5dd4-a804-4e114c60c34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-146972" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3009884b-eaac-58c4-9dd7-476278e483b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2626" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501550} {:answer/id #uuid "43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501590} {:answer/id #uuid "88a07982-8bca-5d34-ba03-ad213b50a5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0808" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501728} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ecc379-ae54-5c46-b4ed-94535e2a2e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501788} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d11df6-7dc5-50f2-bbd9-0d41650103a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501590} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9ea89e-2b67-50ed-9875-e233798706d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146975" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2ffbca-0cac-5940-a7dc-6a3aa9f30b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6954" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501733} {:answer/id #uuid "b739cc9f-d7c6-57b4-96f4-f38acc56c93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501831} {:answer/id #uuid "d46951e0-79b5-5171-aac4-104568cfcf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7924" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501887} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501900} {:answer/id #uuid "e41e3f60-af2a-5d03-ae51-9b94e91c8682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9723" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501900} :answer-field/id #uuid "603f3c7e-5a6d-59bf-b0ec-1681329ffe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-147071" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=99$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c8f58f-95c5-542e-9b35-85744163dc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501535} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fa6a07-689e-56bd-934e-366f80119f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501770} {:answer/id #uuid "c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501856} {:answer/id #uuid "c8967056-a8d1-5db9-a7a2-0afcb5f80f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501864} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d33bab-f28f-555d-b84b-cc1cf9685d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2715" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b58d986a-b956-51c7-a441-3edd459a5e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-147212" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=125$ from a population where $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $65%$ but less than $72%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2266\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2236\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0051\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0049\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0069\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0068\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =P(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.81)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501483} {:answer/id #uuid "32730632-54f9-51bd-9b77-5eb922390acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.47)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501559} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8d336e-ed5a-5688-ade1-9317c97d93ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.97)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501610} {:answer/id #uuid "e4626d23-d099-557f-8b2c-0a74d5d57629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2.11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501926} {:answer/id #uuid "f139c347-a6cc-572a-946f-3b855b388e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.81)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501483} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c885ba5-a648-5bca-b5b2-398b1f373335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147448" :question/problem "An amusement park knows that $25%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501531} {:answer/id #uuid "250e3fc3-8889-5bac-9379-1c96182f3fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-1.62)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501534} {:answer/id #uuid "47f4e987-a1cd-511c-b8f3-f4bb08cf984c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-1.85)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501599} {:answer/id #uuid "7f968a03-d0e7-595f-852d-c5d8937237d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501709} {:answer/id #uuid "d26bcec2-8907-5cfb-9e9c-5911985e9883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a76ecc-0207-5bbe-bd7d-f25da682e7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-147479" :question/problem "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03359e21-7a1b-5dec-803d-c1cd7423ca1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501470} {:answer/id #uuid "45e1015c-d45f-5639-97c2-7d9314f8ad2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501593} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4884" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501703} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4a2dca-96f1-5a35-91e8-35bd1ef6be15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2687" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501846} {:answer/id #uuid "e120a658-b96e-5c48-8cf4-5d70b8be76ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3954" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4884" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501703} :answer-field/id #uuid "f514e27c-296f-5be6-8d4d-2642d4f0a3fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147482" :question/problem "A smartphone app has $500\\,000$ users, $150\\,000$ of which have a paid account. If an app developer randomly samples $300$ users, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $72$ and $90$ (inclusive) users in the sample have a paid account. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5478\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91695da2-de95-5431-872f-19b52844bdd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.53)-Φ(-1.86)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501743} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c38604-6309-551b-ac28-d167af1123ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.23)-Φ(-2.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501772} {:answer/id #uuid "b13dcd98-7237-5fd6-a1e6-940842cbdb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.66)-Φ(-2.23)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501820} {:answer/id #uuid "be8b84ad-a507-55af-aa58-6bcf6faf6c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.55)-Φ(-3.14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501849} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501960} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db11675-d772-5e72-8d9a-3a6e110d5729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" :question/math-academy-id "q-147504" :question/problem "It is known that $35%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
